   'The consumer filed a lawsuit over an online shopping contract concerning the failure to prominently disclose that the food was nearing its expiration date, and the court ruled after considering the nature of the food and the evidence of the transaction.', 'After purchasing dairy food products online, the consumer discovered that they were close to their expiration date without a prominent warning. The food was nearing the end of its shelf life, but the product page did not provide a prominent warning, so the consumer sought a refund and compensation based on the right to know and safety risks. The consumer preserved the order, payment records, unboxing photographs, and communications.', 'The court held that this case should first distinguish between ordinary defects in labeling or advertising and noncompliance rising to the level of food safety. The court held that operators should bear a heightened duty to provide warnings concerning the shelf life and storage conditions. It also assessed the matter in light of the burden of proof, trade practices, and consumers’ reasonable reliance.', 'Regarding the failure to prominently indicate that a product is nearing its expiration date, the court emphasized that the determination should take into account the specific food category, mandatory information disclosure obligations, and the extent to which consumers were actually affected.', '1. Partially support the claims concerning near-expiration products that were not prominently identified; 2. Handle the remaining claims based on the evidence presented.', 'Support', 'Food safety, online shopping, near-expiration products not prominently disclosed, dairy products' );


   'The consumer filed a lawsuit over an online shopping contract dispute concerning unclear ingredient labeling, and the court ruled by taking into account the nature of the food and the transaction evidence.', 'After purchasing a tea beverage product online, the consumer discovered that the ingredient labeling was unclear. The ingredient descriptions on the product page and packaging were inconsistent, giving the consumer reasonable grounds to question the actual ingredients. The consumer retained the order, payment records, unboxing photographs, and communication records.', 'The court held that this case should first distinguish between ordinary defects in labeling or advertising and noncompliance rising to the level of food safety. The court’s determination focused on consumers’ right to know, the completeness of the labeling, and whether safety was affected. It also assessed the matter in light of the burden of proof, trade practices, and consumers’ reasonable reliance.', 'Regarding unclear ingredient labeling, the ruling emphasized that the determination should be made in light of the specific food category, mandatory information disclosure obligations, and the actual extent to which consumers were affected.', '1. The claims concerning unclear ingredient labeling are partially supported; 2. The remaining claims will be handled according to the evidence presented.', 'Support', 'Food safety, online shopping, unclear ingredient labeling, tea beverage' );


   'The consumer filed a lawsuit over an online shopping contract concerning errors or omissions in the nutrition facts table, and the court ruled after considering the nature of the food and the evidence of the transaction.', 'After purchasing snack foods online, the consumer discovered errors and omissions in the nutrition facts panel. The nutrition facts panel contained data-entry errors, and it was necessary to determine whether they constituted defects that neither affected safety nor misled consumers. The consumer retained the order, payment records, unboxing photos, and communication records.', 'The court held that this case should first distinguish ordinary labeling/advertising defects from noncompliance at the food-safety level. The key issue in the ruling was whether the conduct was sufficient to mislead ordinary consumers into making a purchase. It also assessed the matter in light of the burden of proof, customary business practices, and consumers’ reasonable reliance.', 'Regarding errors and omissions in nutrition facts tables, the ruling emphasized that the determination should be made in light of the specific food category, mandatory information disclosure obligations, and the actual extent to which consumers were affected.', '1. The claims concerning errors and omissions in the nutrition facts table are partially supported; 2. The remaining claims will be handled according to the evidence presented.', 'Support', 'Food safety, online shopping, errors and omissions in the nutrition facts table, snacks' );


