   'The consumer filed a lawsuit over an online shopping contract concerning a dispute over the country-of-origin labeling, and the court ruled after considering the nature of the food and the evidence of the transaction.', 'After purchasing dairy food products online, the consumer discovered a dispute concerning the country-of-origin labeling. The country of origin and information about the domestic agent for the imported food had not been fully disclosed, giving rise to a dispute over traceability of the product''s source. The consumer preserved the order, payment records, unboxing photographs, and communications.', 'The court held that this case should first distinguish between ordinary defects in labeling or advertising and noncompliance rising to the level of food safety. The court emphasized the completeness of Chinese-language labels and source information for imported food. It also assessed the matter in light of the burden of proof, trade practices, and consumers’ reasonable reliance.', 'Regarding disputes over country-of-origin labeling, the court emphasized that the determination should take into account the specific food category, mandatory information-disclosure obligations, and the actual extent to which consumers were affected.', '1. The claims concerning the dispute over the indication of origin are partially supported; 2. The remaining claims will be handled according to the evidence presented.', 'Support', 'Food safety, online shopping, disputes over origin labeling, dairy products' );


   'The consumer filed a lawsuit over an online shopping contract concerning the continued sale of food after its recall, and the court ruled after considering the nature of the food and the evidence of the transaction.', 'After purchasing tea-based food products online, the consumer discovered that the products continued to be sold after a recall. Batches that had already been recalled continued to be sold online, and the consumer claimed that the operator was aware of the recall. The consumer kept the order, payment, unboxing photos, and communication records.', 'The court held that this case should first distinguish ordinary labeling/advertising defects from noncompliance at the food-safety level. The ruling centered on the business operator’s actual knowledge and the platform’s duty to take action. It also assessed the matter in light of the burden of proof, customary business practices, and consumers’ reasonable reliance.', 'Regarding the continued sale of products after a recall, the court emphasized that the determination should take into account the specific food category, mandatory information-disclosure obligations, and the actual extent to which consumers were affected.', '1. The claims concerning continued sale after the recall are partially supported; 2. The remaining claims will be handled according to the evidence presented.', 'Support', 'Food safety, online shopping, continued sale after recall, tea beverages' );


   'The consumer filed a lawsuit over an online shopping contract dispute concerning implied efficacy claims on the product page, and the court ruled by taking into account the nature of the food and the transaction evidence.', 'After purchasing snack foods online, the consumer discovered implied efficacy claims on the product page. Ordinary food products implied health or therapeutic effects through images and slogans. The consumer retained the order, payment records, unboxing photos, and communication records.', 'The court held that this case should first distinguish between ordinary defects in labeling or advertising and noncompliance rising to the level of food safety. The court examined whether the overall presentation of the webpage caused consumers to form an expectation that the product could treat disease. It also assessed the matter in light of the burden of proof, trade practices, and consumers’ reasonable reliance.', 'Regarding issues involving efficacy implications on webpages, courts have emphasized that the determination should take into account the specific food category, mandatory information-disclosure obligations, and the actual extent to which consumers were affected.', '1. The claims concerning implied benefits on the product page are partially supported; 2. The remaining claims will be handled according to the evidence presented.', 'Support', 'Food safety, online shopping, implied efficacy claims on webpages, snacks' );


   'The consumer filed a lawsuit over an online shopping contract concerning contamination caused by damage during courier delivery, and the court ruled after considering the nature of the food and the evidence of the transaction.', 'After purchasing a honey product online, the consumer discovered that the package had been damaged and contaminated during delivery. Damage during delivery caused the food packaging to become contaminated, and the dispute concerns the allocation of risk and the burden of proof. The consumer retained the order, payment records, unboxing photographs, and communication records.', 'The court held that this case should first distinguish between ordinary defects in labeling or advertising and noncompliance rising to the level of food safety. The court determined liability by considering the point of delivery, the receipt records, and the condition of the physical goods. It also assessed the matter in light of the burden of proof, trade practices, and consumers’ reasonable reliance.', 'Regarding damage to and contamination of goods during express delivery, the court emphasized that the determination should take into account the specific food category, mandatory information-disclosure obligations, and the actual extent to which consumers were affected.', '1. The claims concerning damage and contamination during courier delivery are partially supported; 2. The remaining claims will be handled according to the evidence presented.', 'Partially upheld', 'Food safety, online shopping, damage and contamination during express delivery, honey' );


